  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 223 PM CDT Sat Oct 24 2026\n\nHigh pressure will continue filtering into the forecast area today \ninto tonight. As a result, winds will continue to diminish this\nafternoon while skies have become mostly sunny. Highs should \nreach the mid 40s to mid 50s. For tonight, clear skies with \nlight and variable winds are expected at most locations through \nmost of the night, though some cloud cover may filter in mainly \nalong and south of I94. Currently, NBM lows are mostly in the \n20s with some low 30s where cloud cover is forecast. However, \ncould see temperatures becoming even colder in areas where skies\ndo in fact remain clear and winds become light and variable.\n\nFor Sunday into Sunday night, surface high reorganizes more in south \ncentral Canada. This should bring increasing cloud cover to southern \nparts of the state where moisture begins to advect in, while \nthe north remains under the influence of high pressure. As such,\nlight precipitation is possible near the ND/SD border on \nSunday, and mainly south of the I94 corridor Sunday night. Any \nprecipitation Sunday should be in the form of rain, though a \nlittle light snow could mix in Sunday night. \n\nMonday is when things become more interesting as aforementioned \nsurface high pressure slides more towards Ontario and even\nmoister air advects into the Dakotas. Simultaneously, models \nare in fair agreement that upper low pressure will develop over \nthe Northern Plains, with South Dakota being the favored \nlocation for the center. This could produce liquid equivalent \nprecipitation in excess of a quarter inch mainly south of I94. \nThe key term there being liquid equivalent. There remains plenty\nof uncertainty such as timing and thermal profiles. However, a \nfew models indicate the potential for accumulating snow Monday \ninto Monday night. Lows Monday morning in the 20s and low 30s \ncould cool the surface enough to make accumulating snow a little\neasier. Furthermore, enhanced snowfall rates could overcome \nwarm surfaces anyways. That said, a solution that maintains \nmostly rain over the area is possible as well. The NBM ensemble \nis producing a greater than 80 percent chance of an inch or more\nof snow mainly south of I94 from Richardton to Medina, though \nthis includes both the daytime Monday and Monday night. These \nprobabilities may be a little high overall given warmer soils \nand the time of day for this time of year, but they certainly \nhighlight the potential that exists for this season's first \nsnowfall.\n\nBeyond Monday night, periodic precipitation chances are within the \nforecast. At this time, these chances are mostly in the form of rain \nas highs Tuesday are mostly in the 40s, then the mid 40s to mid 50s \nthrough the rest of the week. Still, with lows in the 20s and 30s, \ndepending on timing and location, a little snow could mix in at \ntimes as well.",
